			<div class="head">
								<div class="title" id="post-title-4843609">
																	<a href="/forum/t-13909749/exli-apomus-ea#post-4843609">Re: Exli Apomus (EA)</a>
									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dr-hajovsky" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7041744); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7041744&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607845506" alt="Dr Hajovsky" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7041744)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dr-hajovsky" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7041744); return false;" >Dr Hajovsky</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607845349 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Dec 2020 07:42</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13909749/exli-apomus-ea">Exli Apomus (EA)</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4843609">
									

<p>(Can't give greenlights)</p>
<p>Ultimately, the main challenge here is going to be rewriting your virus/parasite to stand out more. The idea of a rampant, incurable, killer virus is already pretty well trod ground in all forms of media. Not to mention, there's already many different infectious virus-like SCPs with many different anomalous effects.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Here's&nbsp;some&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Begone&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The pathogen, discovered in lake Vostok, under the ancient Antarctica ice sheets, is unknown to man, is resistant to all antibodies, and is incurable by any means possible (including anomalous).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Apart from being incurable by both conventional <em>and</em> anomalous means, I just don't see how this particular virus is going to set itself apart. Perhaps giving the virus' victims some kind of anomalous property would be a decent place to start. Or, perhaps the virus spreads itself in some kind of anomalous fashion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After the downfall of the urinary system, it attacks the immune system. It's main target is macrophages and B cells. Once the B cells fall to a critically low amount, it starts another attack on the immune system against the macrophages. They &quot;bundle up&quot; and start overwhelming the macrophages with such intense force that the guard cell just explodes. After the main targets are down, it feeds away at smaller cells, which help them grow in size. The parasites will then feast on blood rapidly, and cause mass blood clots, and stop blood flow almost entirely.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Even if its effects are somewhat gruesome and out of the ordinary, I don't quite see this virus as being anomalous enough. When it comes to strange and somewhat supernatural infections, there's a wide variety of choices. Focusing less on the realistic ways the virus infects the body and more on making the virus unique in the foundation universe will help make this concept successful.</p>
<p>Also, having a narrative is a pretty crucial role when it comes to giving your article legs to stand on. How was the virus discovered? How has the foundation struggled in containing it? Is there a hint that the virus was created by a known entity/GoI? These are just a few stories you can tell throughout the article.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

