						<a href="/forum/t-13897967/hikikomori">Hikikomori</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4843578">
									

<blockquote>
<p>A hikikomori ends up as a SCP entity by means of apparent suicide, becoming capable of entering reality from other people's dreams.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>First thing I will say is that I think using suicide and self harm without substantive reasons why is a bad look, and is a lazy way to show escalation. Yes, hikikomori are known to be susceptible&#8230; but self harm is not a useful part of a story that isn't addressing it in some substantive way. If you're mentioning it because &quot;it fits&quot; or to make it seem edgy, drop it.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>capable of entering reality from other people's dreams</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>What does this mean? Like Freddy Krueger? Or manifesting themselves through dreams from somewhere outside our universe/reality?</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Turns out that the SCP Foundation is protecting the waking reality itself by keeping that very secret.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>How would this be protecting the world? What makes our world less real than someone who originated within our world and became an entity?</em></p>
<ul>
<li>Also&#8230; like these are real people, are you sure you want to exploit the people who live their lives this way for a spooky story?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>What about this anomaly has anything to do with self-imposed isolation? It doesn't feel connected to anything about the anomaly itself. Just someone who was ostracized from society, kills themselves, and then becomes Freddy Kruger (without the horror).</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
					
